---
// Lesson 12.1d — Grid/Symmetry Jam: Playtest
// rpg jam: full playtest rotation — grid-specific tells + one fix.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>12.1d — Grid/Symmetry Jam: Playtest</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The <a href="10.1d.html">10.1d</a> ritual, one last time — with
    grid-specific tells to watch. Symmetry games are the most
    data-friendly jam of the year: every move is an index, every
    death has a cell address, every hesitation happens AT a
    coordinate.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Playtest</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Three rounds, same contracts:</strong> watch
    silently (<a href="2.4.html">2.4</a>), record on a beat
    table, ONE fix, verify, revert on failure. The grid-specific
    tells:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The orientation stall:</strong> tester presses
            up and the cursor moves a direction they did not
            expect. Your y-flip (<a href="12.1c.html">12.1c</a>
            sign) or their mental model? (If two testers stall
            the same way, it is YOUR sign.)</li>
        <li><strong>The symmetry question:</strong> "wait, are we
            playing the same game?" — asked with delight means
            your fairness reads; asked with confusion means the
            mirror is invisible (RENDER it)</li>
        <li><strong>The cell ambiguity:</strong> the tester
            miscounts a gap or misreads adjacency. Grids promise
            precision — visual gaps or soft colors break the
            promise</li>
        <li><strong>Tie-break feels:</strong> the deadlock you
            defined — did the tester experience it as fair? If
            they protest the SAME tie twice, the rule loses
            (change it or make it visible)</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Data, grid edition:</strong> log the CELL of every
    death and every hesitation (column,row). Your playtest.csv
    gains coordinates — and a heatmap of the board falls out for
    free: which cells kill, which cells teach?</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Playtest-day decisions</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>A tester suggests adding a mechanic mid-run. Write
                it down — but why is "put it in the NEXT jam"
                almost always right in the last jam's last
                build day (<a href="10.1e.html">10.1e</a>
                freeze rules apply tomorrow)?</li>
            <li>Your fix made the board readable but killed the
                difficulty. Revert or redesign? Which question
                decides (<a href="3.4.html">3.4</a>: hard vs
                unclear — never fix both at once)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Close</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Update playtest.csv; commit:
                <code>"12.1d: playtest + fix verified"</code>.</li>
            <li>Build + test the BUILD (freeze discipline —
                showcase is <a href="12.1e.html">12.1e</a>).</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Watch for the four grid tells and diagnose each</li>
            <li>Log deaths/hesitations with cell coordinates</li>
            <li>Apply one fix, verify, revert on failure</li>
            <li>Leave with a tested build for showcase day</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../data/playtests.csv">Playtest dataset (CSV)</a> — the format, again</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-12/index.html">Unit 12: Season 2</a> — the ritual, grid edition</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="12.1e.html">12.1e — Grid/Symmetry Jam: Showcase</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="12.1d" title="12.1d Grid/Symmetry Jam: Playtest — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
